cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this code, please cite the paper."
title: "Only one arm's ceiling is estimable: engineered features versus learned sequence representations across 10k to 1.1M claims patients"
authors:
  - family-names: Gabra
    given-names: Daniel
    affiliation: "Schmid College of Science and Technology, Chapman University"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0003-6339-6997"
  - family-names: Rakovski
    given-names: Cyril
    affiliation: "Schmid College of Science and Technology, Chapman University"
    # orcid: add Cyril's iD here once he supplies it
year: 2026
license: MIT
abstract: >-
  Whether learned representations of clinical event sequences can displace
  hand-engineered features is usually settled in the few-shot regime. Using a
  1.25M-patient extraction from a national US commercial claims database, we
  compare 828 engineered features with gradient-boosted trees against a
  Deepr-style 1-D CNN and a BEHRT-style Transformer over identical tokens,
  across a 10k to 1.11M ladder. Engineered features lead at every scale; only
  the feature arm's asymptote is identifiable, and under log-linear gap closure
  parity arrives near 6 to 8 million patients.
